# The Riemann zeta function, zeta(s) = 1 + 1/2^s + 1/3^s + ..., continued to
# the whole complex plane. Its values at the negative integers and at the
# positive even integers are exact rationals, the latter times a power of pi,
# given by the Bernoulli numbers. Its other zeros, the nontrivial ones, lie
# in the strip between real parts 0 and 1; the Riemann hypothesis says they
# all have real part 1/2.
# The program derives the Bernoulli numbers B0 to B12 as exact fractions;
# from them zeta(0) = -1/2, zeta(-1) = -1/12 and zeta(-3) = 1/120, the
# trivial zeros at -2, -4, ..., -10, and Euler's zeta(2) = pi^2/6,
# zeta(4) = pi^4/90 and zeta(6) = pi^6/945. It then evaluates zeta on the
# critical line with Borwein's alternating series and finds it vanishes, to
# within 1e-12, at the first three nontrivial zeros, and not at 1/2 + 20i.
# Shows Logical English arithmetic calling LS functions (exp, log, cos, sin,
# pow, gcd, lcm, floordiv) inside expressions, exact big integers next to
# binary64 floats, and sums and checks by aggregates.
# This checks known zeros numerically; it is evidence for the hypothesis at
# three points, not a proof.
# Sources B. Riemann, Ueber die Anzahl der Primzahlen unter einer gegebenen
#         Groesse (1859); L. Euler, De summis serierum reciprocarum (1740);
#         P. Borwein, An efficient algorithm for the Riemann zeta function
#         (2000); the zeros from A. Odlyzko's tables.

# Indices and factorials, exactly.

0 is an index.
For every N and M, if N is an index and N is less than 80 and M is the value of N + 1 then M is an index.
0 has factorial 1.
For every N and F and M and G,
if N has factorial F and N is less than 80 and M is the value of N + 1 and G is the value of F * M
then M has factorial G.
1 has lcmUpTo 1.
For every N and L and M and K,
if N has lcmUpTo L and N is less than 14 and M is the value of N + 1 and K is the value of lcm(L, M)
then M has lcmUpTo K.

# Bernoulli numbers, with B1 = 1/2, from
# Bm = sum over k of (sum over j of (-1)^j C(k, j) (j + 1)^m) / (k + 1):
# every inner sum is an integer, so the outer one is an integer over
# lcm(1, ..., m + 1), reduced by their gcd.

For every M, if M is an index and M is at most 12 then M is a bernoulliIndex.
For every M and K and F1 and A,
if M is a bernoulliIndex and K is an index and K is at most M and K has factorial F1 and
A is the sum of V for every J and F2 and D and F3 and V such that
(J is an index and J is at most K and J has factorial F2 and D is the value of K - J and D has factorial F3 and
V is the value of pow(-1, J) * floordiv(F1, F2 * F3) * pow(J + 1, M))
then innerSum(M, K, A).
For every M and P and L and N and G and X and Y,
if M is a bernoulliIndex and P is the value of M + 1 and P has lcmUpTo L and
N is the sum of W for every K and A and W such that (innerSum(M, K, A) and W is the value of A * floordiv(L, K + 1)) and
G is the value of gcd(N, L) and X is the value of floordiv(N, G) and Y is the value of floordiv(L, G)
then bernoulli(M, X, Y).

# zeta(-n) = -B(n+1) / (n + 1), and the trivial zeros where it is 0.

For every N and M and P and Q and G and X and Y,
if bernoulli(M, P, Q) and M is greater than 0 and M is at most 11 and N is the value of M - 1 and
G is the value of gcd(P, Q * M) and X is the value of floordiv(0 - P, G) and Y is the value of floordiv(Q * M, G)
then zetaAtNegative(N, X, Y).
For every N and Z,
if zetaAtNegative(N, 0, 1) and Z is the value of 0 - N
then Z is a trivialZero.

# zeta(2n) = (-1)^(n+1) B(2n) 2^(2n-1) / (2n)! times pi^(2n).

For every M and P and Q and N and F and P2 and Q2 and A and B and G and X and Y,
if bernoulli(M, P, Q) and M is greater than 0 and M is at most 6 and N is the value of 2 * M and
N has factorial F and bernoulli(N, P2, Q2) and
A is the value of pow(-1, M + 1) * P2 * pow(2, N - 1) and B is the value of Q2 * F and
G is the value of gcd(A, B) and X is the value of floordiv(A, G) and Y is the value of floordiv(B, G)
then zetaAtEven(N, X, Y).

# Borwein's series for eta(s) = (1 - 2^(1-s)) zeta(s) with n = 40 terms. Its
# weights come from exact integers d0, ..., d40.

For every I and P and F1 and Q and F2 and R and F3 and T,
if I is an index and I is at most 40 and P is the value of 39 + I and P has factorial F1 and
Q is the value of 40 - I and Q has factorial F2 and R is the value of 2 * I and R has factorial F3 and
T is the value of floordiv(40 * F1 * pow(4, I), F2 * F3)
then borweinTerm(I, T).
For every K and D,
if K is an index and K is at most 40 and
D is the sum of T for every I and T such that (borweinTerm(I, T) and I is at most K)
then borweinSum(K, D).
For every K and D and D1 and W,
if borweinSum(K, D) and K is less than 40 and borweinSum(40, D1) and W is the value of (D1 - D) / D1
then borweinWeight(K, W).

# The points: the first three nontrivial zeros, a point on the critical
# line that is not a zero, and s = 2.

point(firstZero, 0.5, 14.134725141734693).
point(secondZero, 0.5, 21.022039638771555).
point(thirdZero, 0.5, 25.01085758014569).
point(notAZero, 0.5, 20.0).
point(two, 2.0, 0.0).

# Each term of eta(s) is (-1)^k w(k) (k + 1)^(-s), and
# (k + 1)^(-s) = e^(-sigma L) (cos(t L) - i sin(t L)) with L = log(k + 1).

For every S and S1 and T and K and W and L and M and X and Y,
if point(S, S1, T) and borweinWeight(K, W) and L is the value of log(K + 1) and
M is the value of pow(-1, K) * W * exp(0 - S1 * L) and
X is the value of M * cos(T * L) and Y is the value of 0 - M * sin(T * L)
then etaTerm(S, K, X, Y).
For every S and S1 and T and R and I and A and C and E and N and Z1 and Z2,
if point(S, S1, T) and
R is the sum of X for every K and X and Y such that etaTerm(S, K, X, Y) and
I is the sum of Y1 for every K1 and X1 and Y1 such that etaTerm(S, K1, X1, Y1) and
A is the value of pow(2, 1 - S1) and C is the value of 1 - A * cos(T * log(2)) and
E is the value of A * sin(T * log(2)) and N is the value of C * C + E * E and
Z1 is the value of (R * C + I * E) / N and Z2 is the value of (I * C - R * E) / N
then zeta(S, Z1, Z2).

# A zero on the critical line, to within 1e-12.

For every S and T and Z1 and Z2 and A,
if point(S, 0.5, T) and zeta(S, Z1, Z2) and A is the value of hypot(Z1, Z2) and A is less than 1e-12
then S is a criticalLineZero.
For every S and T and Z1 and Z2 and A,
if point(S, 0.5, T) and zeta(S, Z1, Z2) and A is the value of hypot(Z1, Z2) and A is at least 1e-12
then S is a nonzeroPoint.

# zeta(2) from the series agrees with pi^2/6 from the Bernoulli numbers.

For every X and Y and Z1 and Z2 and V and D,
if zetaAtEven(2, X, Y) and zeta(two, Z1, Z2) and V is the value of X * pi * pi / Y and
D is the value of abs(Z1 - V) and D is less than 1e-12
then eulerAgrees(2, V, Z1).

Find every M and X and Y such that bernoulli(M, X, Y).
Find every N and X and Y such that zetaAtNegative(N, X, Y).
Find every Z such that Z is a trivialZero.
Find every N and X and Y such that zetaAtEven(N, X, Y).
Find every S such that S is a criticalLineZero.
Find every S such that S is a nonzeroPoint.
Find every N and V and Z such that eulerAgrees(N, V, Z).
